theory:
## Every Function Needs a Unique Name

In some languages, you can have multiple functions with the same name but different parameters (called **overloading**). XIOM doesn't allow this — each function must have a unique name.

### Not Allowed in XIOM

```xiom
// This does NOT work in XIOM:
// fn print_value(x: Int) { ... }
// fn print_value(x: Str) { ... }  // ERROR: duplicate name!
```

### What To Do Instead

Use descriptive, distinct names:

```xiom
fn print_int(x: Int) {
  io.println("Number calculation complete!");
}

fn print_str(x: Str) {
  io.println(x);
}

fn print_bool(x: Bool) {
  if x {
    io.println("true");
  } else {
    io.println("false");
  }
}

fn main() -> Int {
  print_int(42);
  print_str("Hello");
  print_bool(true);
  return 0;
}
```

### Naming Conventions

Use the type or purpose in the name to differentiate:

```xiom
fn add_ints(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { return a + b; }
fn add_floats(a: Float64, b: Float64) -> Float64 { return a + b; }

fn area_of_square(side: Int) -> Int { return side * side; }
fn area_of_rectangle(w: Int, h: Int) -> Int { return w * h; }
```

### Why No Overloading?

XIOM values clarity. When you see `print_int(42)`, there's zero ambiguity about which function is called. This makes code easier to read, debug, and reason about.

### The Bottom Line

Give each function a name that describes exactly what it does. Longer, clearer names beat shorter, ambiguous ones.

